55,3,,status,Ryuji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,1,talk,Midori,,I lean toward Candidate A. A hosted suite with a regional service team provides the single point of accountability we need to avoid the fragmented support mess I found in the outage reports.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,I favor Candidate D. Their focus on transport operators and the presence of an engineering team during rollout is the only way to ensure the system survives our legacy hardware and the lack of a freeze window.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,May,,"I'm leaning toward A. A hosted suite means the vendor handles the updates, so we don't end up with another fossil. Plus, it avoids the nightmare of separate licenses that Benjamin and I spotted.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,1,talk,Misaki,,I favor Candidate D. Having an engineering team present through the rollout is essential given our staffing shortages and the tendency of supervisors to abandon rigid systems. It provides the hands-on support our people need.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"I find Candidate C to be an absolute nightmare in the making; modular licenses are precisely the financial leak I wish to avoid. Between A and D, A's fixed configuration and hosted nature suggest a far more predictable cost structure.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Ryuji, @Misaki, I understand the appeal of D's on-site team, but does that solve the long-term accountability issue? Once the rollout team leaves, we might be back to fragmented support if the system is on our own servers.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, the immediate risk is a total dispatch failure during peak season. A hosted suite like A often requires a standardized environment; D's engineering team can adapt the system to our actual yard hardware and manage the cutover without a freeze.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, you're always thinking about the ""now,"" but what about the ""later""? If we go with D and put it on our own servers, we're just building another fossil that we'll be too scared to update in five years. A is much sleeker.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@May, the ""later"" is exactly why I worry. If we choose a hosted system like A with a fixed configuration, the burden of making it work for our supervisors falls entirely on our remaining administrators. We cannot afford that risk.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, while I appreciate the concern for our weary administrators, we must consider the financial burden of ""on-premise"" solutions. Candidate D's server-based model often hides a mountain of maintenance costs that arrive long after the engineers depart.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Misaki, I see your point, but a fixed configuration might actually be a blessing. It prevents the plan from drifting into a complex mess that no one knows how to manage once the initial excitement fades.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, a fixed configuration is only a blessing if it actually works on the floor. If the ""fixed"" process doesn't account for our legacy scanners, the supervisors will simply return to their spreadsheets.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, you're making it sound like we're choosing between a fancy toy and a rusty tractor! But really, if we go with D, we're just paying for a fancy installation to end up with a system we can't update. A is the way to go.","{""words"":45,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@May, a ""rusty tractor"" that actually works is better than a ""fancy toy"" that our staff can't maintain. If the system is too rigid, the burden of bridging the gap falls on our few remaining administrators.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, @Misaki, your devotion to the ""rusty tractor"" is touching, but let us be rational. Candidate A's hosted model eliminates the server maintenance costs and the ""fossilization"" May fears, providing a far more elegant financial horizon.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Misaki"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I believe Candidate A is the most balanced choice. It solves the accountability gap with a regional service team and prevents the ""fossil"" problem through hosting, ensuring we don't repeat our past mistakes. I vote for A.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I cannot support A. A fixed configuration is too rigid for our fragmented hardware and peak-season constraints. Only D provides the engineering presence necessary to ensure the system actually functions in our yards. I vote for D.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,May,,I'm sticking with A! It's the only one that stops the bleeding from those separate licenses and ensures we aren't just patching a dinosaur for the next decade. Let's go for the sleek option. I vote for A.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Misaki,,I cannot support A. A fixed configuration and a remote service team will not bridge the gap for our exhausted staff and skeptical supervisors. We need the hands-on support D provides. I vote for D.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a classic stalemate between the pragmatists and the visionaries. However, logic dictates that we cannot afford a system that is a financial black hole. Candidate A is the only rational choice. I vote for A.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
